= Accuracy and precision — Simple =

'''Accuracy''' and '''precision''' are not the same thing.

'''Accuracy''' is about how close a measurement is to the correct or reference value.

'''Precision''' is about how closely repeated measurements agree with each other.

Imagine throwing darts.

If all the darts land close together but far from the center, the throws are precise but not accurate.

If the darts are spread out around the center, they are less precise even if the average is close to the target.

A measuring instrument can therefore give almost the same wrong answer every time.

That is why repeating a measurement does not automatically remove a calibration problem.

[[Uncertainty/Simple|Measurement uncertainty]] is another idea. It describes how uncertain the measurement result is, not simply whether the instrument is "accurate."

